EXT. TREEHOUSE IN A SYCAMORE - LATE AFTERNOON

A crooked platform nailed into the branches of an old sycamore. A rope ladder. A tin sign: NO PARENTS. Golden light slanting through leaves.

WINIFRED "WINNIE" OKORO-BAKER, 12, sits cross-legged with a deck of cards. Her best friend, KIERAN PRZYBYLSKI-DIOP, 12, lies on his back chewing a grass blade. On the opposite branch, her older brother AUGUSTINE OKORO-BAKER, 16, strums a ukulele with the lazy patience of someone who isn't practicing. And in the lower fork, with legs dangling, sits a new neighbor, LOTTE ABERNATHY-SATO, 13, clutching a paper bag of licorice and watching all three with wide, uncertain eyes.

WINNIE
Deal me in.

KIERAN
You don't know how to play.

WINNIE
I know enough.

AUGUSTINE
(strumming)
She cheats.

WINNIE
I do not!

AUGUSTINE
^She cheats.

KIERAN
(laughing)
You both said it at the same time.

Winnie throws a card at Augustine. It flutters off the platform and spins to the ground. Lotte leans down to follow its path, then straightens and offers the bag.

LOTTE
Licorice?

WINNIE
(brightening)
Yes. Black or red?

LOTTE
Both.

Kieran sits up. Winnie plucks a red strand. Augustine, still strumming, leans across to take a black one. Lotte smiles, relieved.

KIERAN
You're the new one.

LOTTE
Lotte.

AUGUSTINE
Where'd you move from?

LOTTE
Rotterdam.

WINNIE
Where's that?

LOTTE
Far.

Kieran shuffles the deck. Winnie takes a card, glances at it, then slides it face down. Augustine's chords slow into something sweeter. Lotte tucks her knees up. A breeze lifts the sycamore leaves.

The four of them sit in the green quiet. The ukulele hums. A card drifts down from somewhere above and lands in Lotte's lap. She looks at it, then at the others, and grins.
